---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ResourceCard from '../../../components/ResourceCard.astro';
import resources from '../../../data/resources.json';

const lang = 'en';

const relatedResources = resources.filter(r =>
  r.scienceTags.some(tag => ['porges', 'levine', 'somatic'].includes(tag))
);
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Polyvagal Theory — The Science — Activist Resiliency"
  description="How Stephen Porges' polyvagal theory explains the autonomic nervous system, safety, and why activists experience overwhelm, shutdown, and connection."
  lang={lang}
>
  <div class="page-header">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="breadcrumb"><a href="/en/science">The Science</a> &rsaquo; Polyvagal Theory</p>
      <h1 class="page-title">Polyvagal Theory</h1>
      <p class="page-byline">Research by Stephen Porges &bull; Related work: Peter Levine, Bessel van der Kolk</p>
      <p class="page-intro">
        Developed by neuroscientist Stephen Porges, polyvagal theory offers a
        groundbreaking map of how the autonomic nervous system responds to safety,
        danger, and life threat — and why social connection is our most fundamental
        biological resource for regulation.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <article class="content-article">
    <div class="container">

      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>The Three Branches of the Autonomic Nervous System</h2>
        <p>
          Classical neuroscience described two modes: sympathetic (fight or flight)
          and parasympathetic (rest and digest). Porges identified a third, evolutionarily
          older branch of the vagus nerve — and showed that the three form a hierarchy
          that the nervous system moves through in a predictable sequence.
        </p>

        <div class="three-states">
          <div class="state-card state-ventral">
            <h3 class="state-name">Ventral Vagal</h3>
            <p class="state-subtitle">Social Engagement System</p>
            <p class="state-keywords">Safety &bull; Connection &bull; Regulation</p>
            <p>
              The newest evolutionary branch of the vagus nerve. When activated,
              we feel safe, curious, and able to connect. Our face is expressive,
              our voice is warm, we can listen. This is the state where learning,
              creativity, and collaboration are possible.
            </p>
            <p>
              For activists: grounded presence, clear thinking, the capacity to
              hold complexity, and genuine connection with others in the movement.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="state-card state-sympathetic">
            <h3 class="state-name">Sympathetic</h3>
            <p class="state-subtitle">Fight / Flight / Mobilization</p>
            <p class="state-keywords">Danger &bull; Activation &bull; Action</p>
            <p>
              When the nervous system perceives threat, it mobilizes energy for
              action. Heart rate increases, attention narrows, digestion slows.
              This is adaptive in acute danger — but when chronically activated
              without resolution, it depletes the system.
            </p>
            <p>
              For activists: urgency, anxiety, rage, hypervigilance, difficulty
              sleeping, racing thoughts, reactive decision-making, interpersonal
              conflict within teams.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="state-card state-dorsal">
            <h3 class="state-name">Dorsal Vagal</h3>
            <p class="state-subtitle">Freeze / Collapse / Shutdown</p>
            <p class="state-keywords">Life Threat &bull; Immobilization &bull; Numbness</p>
            <p>
              The oldest evolutionary branch — a last-resort survival response
              when fight or flight have failed. The system shuts down to conserve
              energy. We feel numb, disconnected, hopeless, and unable to act.
            </p>
            <p>
              For activists: numbness to injustice, apathy, inability to organize,
              dissociation, depression, dropping out of movements, cynicism as
              a defense against feeling.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>How This Maps to the Moral Resiliency Framework</h2>
        <p>
          The Moral Resiliency framework underlying this site maps directly onto
          polyvagal theory:
        </p>
        <ul class="mapping-list">
          <li>
            <strong>Ventral vagal = the Resilient Path.</strong> Integration,
            regulation, and flexible response. The capacity to be moved by injustice
            without being swept away — to act from empathy rather than reactivity.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Sympathetic activation = the Reactive Path.</strong>
            Empathic distress, burnout from over-activation, urgency that overrides
            judgment, and interpersonal friction from unregulated emotion.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Dorsal vagal = the Withdrawn Path.</strong> Compassion fatigue,
            numbing, disengagement, moral disengagement — the place where care
            has been frozen out to protect from overwhelm.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          The goal is not to eliminate sympathetic or dorsal responses — they are
          survival adaptations. The goal is to expand the capacity to return to
          ventral vagal: to develop what Porges calls "neuroception" — the ability
          to accurately sense safety — and to strengthen the pathways back to
          connection and regulation.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>Neuroception: The Body's Radar for Safety</h2>
        <p>
          Porges coined the term <em>neuroception</em> to describe the nervous system's
          continuous, below-conscious scanning of the environment for cues of safety
          or threat. This happens faster than thought — which is why our bodies can
          respond to perceived danger before we're conscious of it.
        </p>
        <p>
          Cues of safety (the sound of a warm voice, a relaxed face, physical
          stillness, familiar smells) activate ventral vagal pathways. Cues of threat
          (conflict, loud noise, facial expressions of contempt, physical constriction)
          activate sympathetic or dorsal responses.
        </p>
        <p>
          For activists, whose environments are often saturated with cues of threat
          — confrontational spaces, hostile authority, media that amplifies harm —
          the nervous system can get stuck in high alert. Practices that deliberately
          introduce cues of safety (slow breathing, connection, grounding, movement)
          help restore regulatory capacity.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>The Vagal Brake and Why Practices Work</h2>
        <p>
          The ventral vagal system acts as a "vagal brake" that modulates sympathetic
          arousal. When the vagal tone is strong, we can tolerate higher levels of
          activation without tipping into dysregulation. We can be in a heated meeting
          and not lose ourselves. We can hold space for someone in crisis without
          absorbing their distress.
        </p>
        <p>
          Research shows that vagal tone is not fixed. It is trainable. Practices
          like slow diaphragmatic breathing, humming, chanting, cold water exposure,
          and face-to-face connection all increase vagal tone. This is why the
          practices in the Build Resilience section are not merely self-care
          strategies — they have measurable effects on nervous system regulation.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="content-section resources-section">
        <h2>Related Resources</h2>
        <p class="resources-intro">
          The following resources draw on polyvagal theory, somatic trauma approaches
          (Peter Levine), and body-based resilience practices.
        </p>
        <div class="resources-grid">
          {relatedResources.map(resource => (
            <ResourceCard resource={resource} lang={lang} />
          ))}
        </div>
      </section>

    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .page-header {
    padding: var(--space-xl) 0 var(--space-md);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-sage-light);
  }

  .breadcrumb {
    font-size: var(--font-size-sm);
    color: var(--color-charcoal-light);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-sm);
  }

  .breadcrumb a {
    color: var(--color-sage);
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .breadcrumb a:hover {
    text-decoration: underline;
  }

  .page-title {
    font-family: var(--font-heading);
    font-size: var(--font-size-2xl);
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--color-charcoal);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-xs);
  }

  .page-byline {
    font-size: var(--font-size-sm);
    color: var(--color-charcoal-light);
    font-style: italic;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-sm);
  }

  .page-intro {
    font-size: var(--font-size-base);
    color: var(--color-charcoal);
    line-height: var(--line-height-body);
    max-width: var(--content-width);
    opacity: 0.85;
  }

  .content-article {
    padding: var(--space-xl) 0 var(--space-2xl);
  }

  .content-section {
    max-width: var(--content-width);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-xl);
  }

  .content-section h2 {
    font-family: var(--font-heading);
    font-size: var(--font-size-xl);
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--color-charcoal);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-md);
  }

  .content-section p {
    font-size: var(--font-size-base);
    line-height: var(--line-height-body);
    color: var(--color-charcoal);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-sm);
  }

  .content-section p:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .three-states {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    gap: var(--space-md);
    margin-top: var(--space-md);
  }

  .state-card {
    border-radius: 10px;
    padding: var(--space-md);
    border-left: 5px solid transparent;
  }

  .state-card p {
    font-size: var(--font-size-sm);
    line-height: 1.7;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-xs);
  }

  .state-ventral {
    background-color: #eef4ec;
    border-left-color: var(--color-sage);
  }

  .state-sympathetic {
    background-color: #fdf0ec;
    border-left-color: var(--color-clay);
  }

  .state-dorsal {
    background-color: #f0eef5;
    border-left-color: #7a6a9a;
  }

  .state-name {
    font-family: var(--font-heading);
    font-size: var(--font-size-base);
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--color-charcoal);
    margin-bottom: 0.2rem;
  }

  .state-subtitle {
    font-size: var(--font-size-sm);
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--color-charcoal-light);
    margin-bottom: 0.25rem;
  }

  .state-keywords {
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    color: var(--color-charcoal-light);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-sm);
  }

  .mapping-list {
    padding-left: var(--space-md);
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-sm);
    margin: var(--space-sm) 0;
  }

  .mapping-list li {
    font-size: var(--font-size-base);
    line-height: var(--line-height-body);
  }

  .resources-section {
    max-width: none;
  }

  .resources-section h2 {
    max-width: var(--content-width);
  }

  .resources-intro {
    max-width: var(--content-width);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-md) !important;
  }

  .resources-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(280px, 1fr));
    gap: var(--space-md);
  }
</style>
